No answer leak detected. I'll solve directly. (I interpret $C$ as the Cantor set, the standard denotation; the construction in fact works for any perfect $C\subseteq\mathbb{R}$, and more generally.)

---

**Claim.** There exists a one-to-one function $f:C\to\mathbb{R}$ whose graph $\Gamma_f$ is dense in $C\times\mathbb{R}$.

**Proof.**

The argument has two stages: (1) prescribe the values of $f$ on a carefully chosen countable subset of $C$ so that the graph already meets every nonempty basic open subset of $C\times\mathbb{R}$; (2) extend injectively to all of $C$.

---

**Stage 1. A countable dense subset of $C\times\mathbb{R}$ with distinct coordinates.**

Since $C\times\mathbb{R}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^2$ is a separable metric space, it has a countable base $\mathcal{B}=\{B_1,B_2,\dots\}$ of nonempty open sets. We choose points $(x_n,y_n)\in B_n$ inductively so that

- all the $x_n$ are pairwise distinct, and
- all the $y_n$ are pairwise distinct.

Suppose $(x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_{n-1},y_{n-1})$ have been chosen. The "forbidden" set inside $B_n$ is
$$F_n \;=\; \bigl(\{x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}\}\times\mathbb{R}\bigr)\;\cup\;\bigl(C\times\{y_1,\dots,y_{n-1}\}\bigr),$$
i.e. the union of finitely many vertical lines $\{x_i\}\times\mathbb{R}$ and finitely many horizontal lines $C\times\{y_i\}$. Each such line is closed and has empty interior in $C\times\mathbb{R}$: a vertical line $\{x_i\}\times\mathbb{R}$ has empty interior because $C$ has no isolated points (the Cantor set is perfect), so every neighbourhood of $(x_i,t)$ contains points with a different first coordinate; a horizontal line $C\times\{y_i\}$ has empty interior because $\mathbb{R}$ has no isolated points. A finite union of closed nowhere-dense sets is nowhere dense, hence $F_n$ does not cover the nonempty open set $B_n$. Pick
$$(x_n,y_n)\in B_n\setminus F_n.$$
Then $x_n\notin\{x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}\}$ and $y_n\notin\{y_1,\dots,y_{n-1}\}$, as required.

The resulting set $D=\{(x_n,y_n):n\ge 1\}$ is dense in $C\times\mathbb{R}$: every nonempty open set contains some $B_n$, and $B_n\ni(x_n,y_n)\in D$.

---

**Stage 2. Defining $f$ and extending injectively.**

Define $f$ on the countable set $S=\{x_n:n\ge 1\}\subseteq C$ by
$$f(x_n)=y_n\qquad(n\ge 1).$$
Because the $x_n$ are distinct this is well defined, and because the $y_n$ are distinct it is injective on $S$.

It remains to extend $f$ injectively to all of $C$. The remaining domain is $C\setminus S$ and the remaining codomain is $\mathbb{R}\setminus\{y_n:n\ge 1\}$. We have
$$|C\setminus S|\;\le\;|C|\;\le\;\mathfrak c, \qquad \bigl|\mathbb{R}\setminus\{y_n\}\bigr|\;=\;\mathfrak c,$$
the latter because removing a countable set from $\mathbb{R}$ leaves a set of cardinality $\mathfrak c$. Hence there exists an injection
$$g:\;C\setminus S\;\longrightarrow\;\mathbb{R}\setminus\{y_n:n\ge 1\}.$$
Define
$$f(x)=\begin{cases} y_n & x=x_n\in S,\\ g(x) & x\in C\setminus S.\end{cases}$$
The two pieces take values in the disjoint sets $\{y_n\}$ and $\mathbb{R}\setminus\{y_n\}$, each piece is injective, so $f:C\to\mathbb{R}$ is injective overall.

---

**Density of the graph.**

The graph $\Gamma_f=\{(x,f(x)):x\in C\}$ contains $D=\{(x_n,y_n)\}$, since $f(x_n)=y_n$. As $D$ is dense in $C\times\mathbb{R}$, so is $\Gamma_f\supseteq D$. Concretely: any nonempty basic open set $U\times V\subseteq C\times\mathbb{R}$ contains some $B_n$, hence contains $(x_n,y_n)=(x_n,f(x_n))\in\Gamma_f$.

---

Therefore a one-to-one function $f:C\to\mathbb{R}$ with $\Gamma_f$ dense in $C\times\mathbb{R}$ **does exist**. $\blacksquare$

